            <ArticleTitle>Limb ischemia due to use of internal thoracic artery in coronary bypass.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>254-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Immediately after undergoing coronary bypass grafting using the left internal thoracic artery, a 59-year-old man developed left leg ischemia. Right-to-left femoral artery crossover bypass was performed and the ischemia resolved. A 72-year-old man developed left calf pain 12 days after a similar procedure; peripheral angiography revealed stenosis of the abdominal aorta and distal peripheral arteries, which did not require intervention.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
